= Solution
{parent=1}

Let $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ be distinct elements of a <finite field> $\mathbb F_q$, with $1\leq k\leq n$. The <Reed-Solomon code> consists of the evaluation <vectors>
$$
\operatorname{RS}_k(a)=\{(f(a_1),\ldots,f(a_n)):f\in\mathbb F_q[X],\ \deg f<k\}.
$$
Evaluation is an <injective> <linear map>: a nonzero <polynomial> of degree at most $k-1$ cannot vanish at all $n$ distinct points. Thus the <dimension> is $k$. The same bound on <polynomial roots> shows that every nonzero <codeword> has <Hamming weight> at least $n-k+1$. Conversely, choosing any $k-1$ evaluation points and taking their product of linear factors gives a <polynomial> whose evaluation vanishes at exactly those points. Its <Hamming weight> is $n-k+1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{d=n-k+1.}
$$
For completeness, deleting $d-1$ coordinates from a <linear code> of <minimum Hamming distance> $d$ is injective on <codewords>, since two words with the same retained coordinates would differ in fewer than $d$ positions. Therefore $q^k\leq q^{n-d+1}$, giving the <Singleton bound> $d\leq n-k+1$. The <Reed-Solomon code> attains this bound and is a <maximum distance separable code>. Multiplying individual coordinates by nonzero <field> elements changes neither the <dimension> nor the <Hamming weight>, so the same proof applies to a <generalized Reed-Solomon code>.

To specify the <generator polynomials of a cyclic code> unambiguously, use the <primitive cyclic Reed-Solomon code> convention. Put $n=q-1$, choose a <primitive element of a finite field> $\omega$, and set
$$
C_{k,b}=\langle g_b(X)\rangle\subseteq\mathbb F_q[X]/(X^n-1),\qquad
 g_b(X)=\prod_{t=b}^{b+n-k-1}(X-\omega^t).
$$
Exponents are taken modulo $n$. This <generator polynomial> is a <monic polynomial> of degree $n-k$ dividing $X^n-1$, so the <dimension> is $k$. It agrees with the evaluation form
$$
C_{k,b}=\{(a_j^{1-b}f(a_j))_{j=0}^{n-1}:\deg f<k\},\qquad a_j=\omega^j.
$$
Indeed, at a defining exponent $t=b,\ldots,b+n-k-1$, a monomial $f(X)=X^s$ contributes the <finite geometric series> $\sum_j a_j^{t+1-b+s}$. Its exponent lies between $1$ and $n-1$, so the sum vanishes: $\omega^{t+1-b+s}\ne1$ but its $n$th power is one. Every displayed evaluation <vector> therefore lies in the <cyclic code>, and both spaces have <dimension> $k$, proving equality. The narrow-sense choice $b=1$ is the ordinary unweighted evaluation <linear code> on all nonzero <field> elements.

The <duality of primitive cyclic Reed-Solomon codes> now follows directly. A <vector> in $C_{k,b}$ has coordinates $a_j^{1-b}f(a_j)$, while a <vector> in $C_{n-k,1-b}$ has coordinates $a_j^b h(a_j)$, with $\deg h<n-k$. Their <dot product> is $\sum_j a_j f(a_j)h(a_j)$. Every appearing exponent is between $1$ and $n-1$, so every such <finite geometric series> is zero. The two spaces are orthogonal and their <dimensions> sum to $n$, giving
$$
\boxed{C_{k,b}^{\perp}=C_{n-k,1-b}.}
$$
Thus the full cyclic family is closed under <dual codes>. The offset matters: the <dual code> of the narrow-sense <linear code> generally has offset zero, rather than one.

For arbitrary evaluation points, the precise statement is closure of <generalized Reed-Solomon codes> under duality. If $C=\operatorname{GRS}_k(a,v)$, put $P(X)=\prod_i(X-a_i)$ and $u_i=(v_iP'(a_i))^{-1}$. <Lagrange interpolation> shows $\sum_i p(a_i)/P'(a_i)=0$ whenever $\deg p\leq n-2$: this sum is the coefficient of $X^{n-1}$ in the interpolation formula for $p$. Taking $p=fh$ with $\deg f<k$ and $\deg h<n-k$ proves orthogonality of $C$ and $\operatorname{GRS}_{n-k}(a,u)$. Comparing <dimensions> gives equality with $C^\perp$. An unweighted evaluation <linear code> at arbitrary points need not have an unweighted evaluation dual at the same points; the multiplier form and the cyclic offset formula resolve that convention issue.

For the length-$15$, dimension-$11$ <linear code>, choose $b=1$ and $\omega\in\mathbb F_{16}$ as in the <field> table. Then $d=15-11+1=5$ and
$$
g_1(X)=\prod_{t=1}^4(X-\omega^t).
$$
The <field characteristic> is two, and the table gives $\omega^4=\omega+1$. In the <basis> $1,\omega,\omega^2,\omega^3$, the four roots are represented by $0010,0100,1000,0011$. Their elementary symmetric functions are
$$
\begin{aligned}
s_1&=\omega+\omega^2+\omega^3+\omega^4=\omega^{13},\\
s_2&=\omega^3+\omega^4+\omega^5+\omega^5+\omega^6+\omega^7=\omega^6,\\
s_3&=\omega^6+\omega^7+\omega^8+\omega^9=\omega^3,\\
s_4&=\omega^{1+2+3+4}=\omega^{10}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The repeated terms in $s_2$ cancel because the <field characteristic> is two. There are no alternating minus signs in this characteristic, so
$$
\boxed{g_1(X)=\omega^{10}+\omega^3X+\omega^6X^2+\omega^{13}X^3+\omega^0X^4,\qquad d=5.}
$$
For example, the coefficient <vectors> in ascending order are $0111,1000,1100,1101,0001$, giving the required single powers of $\omega$.

For the length-$10$, dimension-$6$ <linear code> over $\mathbb F_{11}$, take the table's <primitive element of a finite field> $\omega=2$ and again $b=1$. The four roots are $2,4,8,5$. Expanding in $\mathbb F_{11}[X]$ gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
g_2(X)&=(X-2)(X-4)(X-8)(X-5)\\
&=(X^2+5X+8)(X^2+9X+7)\\
&=X^4+3X^3+5X^2+8X+1.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{g_2(X)=1+8X+5X^2+3X^3+X^4,\qquad d=10-6+1=5.}
$$
The coefficients are reduced modulo $11$, including the constant term $2\cdot4\cdot8\cdot5=1\pmod{11}$.

A suitable two-error-correcting <Reed-Solomon code> over $\mathbb F_{16}$ is precisely the first <linear code>:
$$
\boxed{[n,k,d]_{16}=[15,11,5]_{16},\qquad g(X)=g_1(X).}
$$
Two distinct <codewords> cannot both be within <Hamming distance> two of a received word, because the <triangle inequality> would put them at distance at most four. Thus this <linear code> corrects any two symbol errors. Its <generator polynomial> has four consecutive <defining zeros of a cyclic code>, and its <dimension> and exact <minimum Hamming distance> have already been proved.

